// PORT SEMANTICS 4/6 -- `.[<numeric>]` is a real offset.
//
// Before the canonical access-path grammar, every model-port segment became a `Symbol`, so a
// port `.[12]` was `Symbol("[12]")` and could never match the `Offset(12)` a binary frontend
// emits. It is now a real `PathSegment::Offset`, and this is the end-to-end half of that: the
// encoding is pinned by `models_loading.rs::offset_port_produces_a_real_offset`, the taint
// semantics here.
//
// `Argument(0).[12] -> Return` maps `t.[12].X` to `u.X`, so it unwraps the offset exactly the
// way `port_in_symbol.tnt`'s named field unwraps a level.
//
// NOTE for lua and tree-sitter C: a source-level `t[12]` is the *symbol* `[12]` there, so a port
// aimed at it is `.\[12]`, escaped. Unescaped, as here, it is an offset and matches pcode only.
def id(v): 1
where summaries [return <- v.[12]]
{
start:
  w = "nothing";
  return w;
}

def main() {
start:
  t.[12].g = source(Secret);
  u = id(t);
  // Predicted: u.g -- the port consumed the offset.
  probe = u.g;
  sink(probe, Secret);
  // NOT at the offset: it is gone, not preserved.
  deeper = u.[12].g;
  errsink(deeper, Secret);
  return;
}
